WEBVTT

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:30.530
<v Speaker 1>Good morning, good afternoon, good evening and good night. Wherever you are in the world, you are extremely welcome to our highlights package for Proto Honolulu 2009 with me, your host Rich Hagen. Honolulu was the second stop on the Pro Tour this year and with Kyoto already in the books, it would be hard pressed to top an exceptional event from Japan. There Standard took centre stage and so did Frenchman Gabriel Nassif</v>

00:00:30.630 --> 00:01:01.180
<v Speaker 1>who survived a top deck or no moment against England's Matteo Orsini Jones to go on and defeat the hottest man in magic, Louis Scott Vargas in the final. Despite then winning Grand Prix Chicago, Nasif and his rival Scott Vargas remained almost inseparable in the Player of the Year race with the past 2 champions 2008 Shuhei Nakamura and Tomoharu Saito of Japan in 2007 holding 3rd and 4th places. Saito had brought himself into contention by winning successive</v>

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:31.950
<v Speaker 1>Grand Prix and this Honolulu Pro Tour came in the wake of two Grand Prix with a trip to Brazil to follow on home turf. At Grand Prix Barcelona, Joe Calafell used an extraordinary 41 land Seismic Assault deck to take the title, while Frenchmen Yan Massacre warped the meta game seven days later at a hugely well attended Grand Prix Seattle Tacoma. Now standard decks would be set aside as the best players and deck builders in the world converged on the Hawaiian island of Oahu,</v>

00:01:32.030 --> 00:02:03.350
<v Speaker 1>just a stones throw from Waikiki Beach. Like all the Pro tours on the 2009 schedule, Honolulu featured a split format. In Kyoto, there were 14 rounds before the cut top 8. This time that would be extended by an additional 2 rounds, allowing just that little bit more room for manoeuvre and pretty much guaranteeing that anyone who managed to ace day 2 would find themselves with a seat at the final table. The tournament would begin with five rounds of block constructed. The first day would end with all players</v>

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:34.290
<v Speaker 1>eligible to participate in three rounds of Shards of Alara Conflux Alara Reborn Draft with 15 points or five match wins out of the 8 required to progress today. Two that second day with roughly 2/3 of the field gone would begin with another set of three round draft pods, and then five rounds of Block Constructed would leave us with the inevitable 8 for Sunday action. With Honolulu officially designated the Draft Pro Tour for the season, this meant that it would be the 40 rather than the 60 card decks</v>

00:02:34.380 --> 00:03:06.080
<v Speaker 1>that would determine the champion. But as 396 players sat down for round one, even the first draft of the tournament was some hours away. Instead, players had to focus on the vagaries of block Constructed. With Magic Online switching on Alara Reborn only a few days before the event, there was huge interest among the pro community as to which decks would at least tentatively come out on top in online premier event action. It turns out there was nothing tentative about it. Before the arrival of the Laura Reborn,</v>

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:36.510
<v Speaker 1>the red green white Shard of Naya was a dominant force. Suddenly it was all junked. And for once that word all isn't too far from the truth. While the precise makeup of the decks varied from full on aggression, the so-called Junk Hack Blade dot deck, named after the Super aggressive 2 drop two more controlling variants. One card looked to be absolutely defining the format. That card was an initially innocuous looking forecasting cost spell by the name of Bloodbraid Elf. With three power</v>

00:03:36.580 --> 00:04:07.040
<v Speaker 1>and just two toughness, 4 manner didn't seem that much of A deal, but the Elf was rather speedy, coming into play with haste and that put it into a bracket with plenty of other three power hasty men that had seen play in various formats down the years. What made this card so spectacular was the new Alara Reborn keyword that had struck fear into the hearts of many. That word was Cascade. With the Bloodbraid Elf still on the stack, players got to reveal cards from the top of their library until a card with a lower converted mana cost,</v>

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:37.190
<v Speaker 1>in this case 3 or less, was revealed. At that point they got to play that revealed spell for free. It said that most good Magic players three favorite words are draw a card, but free spells is definitely high on the list, especially when with a little bit of constructed deck building creativity you could have at least some kind of control over what spells you might be getting for free. High on the list of candidates in the Buy one, Get One free sale were point removal spells like Terminate at 2 manner</v>

00:04:37.360 --> 00:05:07.560
<v Speaker 1>and Maelstrom Pulse at 3. The Pulse had been seen to be extremely efficient at dealing with multiple tokens in standard over the course of the two preceding Grand Prix weekends, making Bitterblossom Fairies, Cloud Goat Ranges, and Spectral Processions significantly less exciting. But as always in the pro world, there was a meta game developing all of its own. Two years ago, coming into Proto Yokohama, Magic Online delivered a resounding verdict. White Weenie was so clearly the best egg,</v>

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:37.750
<v Speaker 1>there was nothing out there to touch it. Just days before the Pro Tour began, 7 out of eight decks in the top eight of a Magic Online Premier event featured the marauding Knights of Time Spiral block. 6 days later, and the highest place White Weenie finisher in the Block Pro Tour was piloted by future World Champion Anti Mallin. He came 50th, not 5th, 50th. See, what tends to happen at the pro level is that everyone runs scared of playing what is, by common consent, the Capital B Capital D</v>

00:05:37.840 --> 00:06:09.860
<v Speaker 1>best deck, because if you expect a huge amount of one particular deck, it's relatively easy to find an answer to that deck. In the case of Yokohama, the mono red decks with protection from white monsters and instant speed sulfur elementals more than took care of business against the little white men. More crucially though, those red decks were flat out good in their own right. Coming into Honolulu, however, although nobody was keen to play the Jump deck, there were very few signs that the protocol as a whole had worked out how to beat it.</v>

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:39.880
<v Speaker 1>That pretty much guaranteed that there would be plenty of black, red, green decks being submitted on Friday morning. Although players had plenty of different ideas about what they should play, one thing hadn't changed since Yokohama the desire to make sure that you beat the best egg. That led to some extremely curious trading exchanges in the couple of days leading up to the event, and some apparently strange maindeck choices. Take a card like Algae Gharial, for example, starting out as a miserable 11 for four manner. That's right,</v>

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:46.520
<v Speaker 1>A11 for four manner. What joy. The Gariel got bigger each time a creature went to the graveyard.</v>

00:06:47.210 --> 00:07:17.050
<v Speaker 1>OK, how about Uril the Miststalker? Now that's the kind of card that separates the men from the boys. Because only those of you have played magic a lot in recent times know what a real actually does. He's a mythic rare, weighing in AT55 for five manner, and he has an ability that makes him get +2, +2 for each aura attached to it. Ohh yes, and nobody at the pro tour used it for this reason. Then there was wall of denial, a real throwback to the old days of wolves, when men were men and wolves were wolves. Not</v>

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:47.900
<v Speaker 1>offenders at 08, The Wall of Denial wasn't about to get rid of opposing armies anytime soon, but boy were they ever nullified with flying. Nothing was getting by the Wall, just like Uriel and the Algae Gariel players were using the Wall for another reason. You see what these three cards had in common? Although not technically, since you could target your own. Uriel was the keyword Shroud. That meant that all those lovely free Terminates and Maelstrom Pulses and Bituminous Blasts,</v>

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:59.630
<v Speaker 1>well, they'd have nowhere to go. And watching URLs leave trade folders like it was going out of fashion was one of the surest signs that playing Jund wouldn't necessarily be the way to go. Once the tournament got under way</v>

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:32.430
<v Speaker 1>after five rounds, we had our first look at the block constructed meta game. 12 players had navigated their way to perfect records and half of these represented the home team. Although with Honolulu almost six hours away from even the West Coast of the mainland USA, it was still a hefty trip for most participants. Eric De Luca, Geoff Pika, and Jim Davis were joined by Zach Hill, Matt Hanson and Brian Kibler. Kibler had been a long time pro before leaving the game for a few years, but it was clear from his all white Rodeo Dr. bought suit</v>

00:08:32.530 --> 00:09:04.020
<v Speaker 1>that he meant business on day one and was keen to make the most of a proto qualifier win that got him an invite to the big show. There were plenty of people rooting for Zach Hill who had moved from hot bed of magic Madison, WI to Kuala Lumpur in Malaysia week by agonizing week. His tales of PTQ near Missus grew and grew in his column OnStar City and his relief and joy were evident in equal measure when he finally got the fabled and indeed entirely fictitious blue envelope. Another man back on the tour after a substantial break</v>

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:34.090
<v Speaker 1>was Scott Dougie Penman, while Christoph Huber was showing no rustiness in compiling the perfect start, having been part of the World Team Champions from Switzerland in 2007. At New York, the European contingent was rounded up by Sebastian Tyler, who was looking to make a second top eight in three attempts, the former Rookie of the Year having taken the mighty Elves to Sunday action in Berlin last year. The top dozen were rounded out by a sum of Fajita and 2006 World Champion Makuhita Mahara of Japan,</v>

00:09:34.320 --> 00:10:04.470
<v Speaker 1>plus little known Indonesian Taufik Andrico Rosamma. In all, plenty of archetypes were represented accurately, reflecting the overall desire to Vanquish the Bloodbraid Elf decks. These included 4 copies of Naujan Control, 3 copies of a full on five colour control strategy that featured Cruel Ultimatum, one NIA Control, 1 Sedrak Sis Agro, 1 Jund Aggro, Defying the Hate and two inventive decks, Kibler's Esper Aggro that included Blasphemes constantly going to the air,</v>

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:36.120
<v Speaker 1>there's decent sized flies and Mahara's Green White Aggro that would ultimately come to represent the new best deck in the format. In total 76 players were able to move to the first draft with one loss or less against them, while at the other end of the table 56 players knew that even with a sweep of their draft pod they would not be featuring on day 2. These included Kyoto top eight man Cedric Phillips, World Team semifinalist Ruiz Guillem de Micheli of Brazil, Spain's Joel Calafell, Ari Lax fresh from the Seattle Grand Prix</v>

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:48.530
<v Speaker 1>top eight former Invitational winner Tiago Chan, World's runner up Jamie Park, and perhaps most surprisingly, the ultra consistent power Lavita Dama de Rosa. No top 8 for them and no Day 2 either.</v>

00:10:50.800 --> 00:11:20.310
<v Speaker 1>As day one came to a close, the 12 that had started out undefeated in block had become just two. Both were from America and both were big names in the game. Brian Kibler and Zach Hill, Tarla Hansen and the Danish pro Rasmus Sibast Romang those in close contention at 7:00 and 1:00, and the man who had finished runner up behind Gillenwater Tapper. In the Block Constructed Pro Tour in Yokohama, Japan's Kazuya Mitamura had aced his first draft pod to move himself to within three points of the lead.</v>

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:51.850
<v Speaker 1>Also on the 7:00 and 1:00 mark was Louisiana resident Tom Ross, surpassing his own expectations. As for the BIG4 in the Player of the Year race, Louis Scott Vargas had been largely anonymous through the first day on route to compiling 62. Gabriel Nassif had started out badly in Block Constructed with A23 record, which meant he had to ace his opening pod to survive the cut. He did so, but remained on the very edge of contention at 5:00 and 3:00. Both the Japanese players, Cito and Nakamura, were also on the minimum required</v>

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:57.790
<v Speaker 1>15 points, and it would be interesting to see which, if any of them could gather momentum on the second day.</v>

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:31.100
<v Speaker 1>It didn't take long for Zach Hill, the last remaining player with a perfect record, to lose that mark early on Day 2. After the second draft, 2 Japanese players joined America's Tom Ross with just a single loss. Kentarou Nonaka was the newcomer. Kazuya Mitamura certainly was not. After one and two records in their second draft, Kibler and Hill both sat on 27 points. Alongside them were a group of Americans featuring Donald Kassner, Conley Woods, Paul Reitzel, Matt Hanson</v>

00:12:31.190 --> 00:13:01.810
<v Speaker 1>and the Magic online star F Freak, performing here under his real name of Brad Nelson. Joining them on the 9:00 and 2:00 mark were Europeans Rasmus Sebaste and Christoph Gregoire, the Belgian player having spent the week closeted with a group of fellow pros that included compatriots Jan Doors and Fried Mulders. Together with a group of relative newcomers and the Dutch star Frank Karsten on eight wins and three losses, the big names were starting to queue up for a run for the top 8. Hall of Famer Rafael Levy of France stood in 17th.</v>

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:32.230
<v Speaker 1>Macuahuitl Mahara and Katara Otsuka represented Japan. Helmut Summers Burger of Austria and the Swede Kenny Oberg were among the European contingent. Gabriel Nassif had fought his way all the way back from 2:00 and 3:00 to 8:00 and 3:00 clean, sweeping both his draft pods, and he kept pace with Louis Scott Vargas, also at 8:00 and 3:00. Following five one in draft. In total eight men, Conley, Woods, Naseef, Nonaka, Brian Boss, Zach Hall, Mitamura, Ross and Kasner managed</v>

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:40.310
<v Speaker 1>perfect draft records, but only three of these would ultimately come through the remaining rounds of block. Constructed to draft again on Sunday.</v>

00:13:43.340 --> 00:14:13.650
<v Speaker 1>Five more rounds of block brought us the unusual situation of all four of the top tables choosing to intentionally draw the final round. Although England's Mark Glenister could, with a significant prevailing wind, have overturned A tiebreak deficit by defeating his last round opponent, it was Brad Nelson who emerged triumphant from that shootout, leaving the online maestro in the cruelest place of all. 9th. The Cup then was to a straight 37 points and up, representing a total of 12 wins, a draw and just three losses.</v>

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:44.360
<v Speaker 1>There could be no doubt that all eight had earned their spots in the Sunday showdown, but it was a largely inexperienced group that would come back to decide the championship. Heading the standings was Belgian Christoff Gregoire on 38 points. Second seed was American Paul Reitzel. He had a decent quantity of propoints and a great pedigree born of many years in the company of your move. Gamers such as Hall of Famers Rob Doherty and Darwin Castle, despite their four and two records in draft Block constructed, have done enough to get Zach Hill and Brian Kibler into the top</v>

00:14:44.440 --> 00:15:14.590
<v Speaker 1>right. Hale had been runner up behind Shuhei Nakamura at Grand Prix Saint Louis in 2006, while Kibler was the only man in the line up to have a premier event victory to his name, winning Grand Prix Toronto in 1997 and again entering the winner's circle in 2002. The fifth seed was Kazuya Mitamura, determined to go 1 better than in Yokohama. Tom Ross had held himself together admirably and Conley Woods had continued the momentum he'd gained with a perfect six ANO draft record to become the 5th</v>

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:45.540
<v Speaker 1>American at the top 8, something that hadn't happened for some years. The final member of the final table was in some ways the most interesting since Michael Hedtke was from the Czech Republic, a part of the world that has been putting together a formidable squad of talent that includes the likes of Martin User, Andra Pasola, Arnold Zidek, Lucas Craft and Blachon, plus near neighbor from the Slovak Republic, Matey Zatoki, the runner up from Pro Tour Berlin. Although virtually unknown, his national pedigree boded well for Hedtke,</v>

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:54.510
<v Speaker 1>and so the block decks were put away and Cascade would become a limited mechanic for the final day. Let's take you to Super Sunday</v>

00:15:56.870 --> 00:16:01.870
<v Speaker 1>quarter final one, Kazuya Mitamura versus Christoph Gregoire.</v>

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:33.020
<v Speaker 1>Mitsumura came in with a red green deck that briefly touched black. With eight forests, 7 mountains, and two swamps, he wasn't exactly relying on technology for his Manor fixing, although Sylvan Bounty, Fiery Fall, and Trace of Abundance would help. After an opening Drumhunter, he picked up back-to-back Vivian Stingers, and the artifact quieter Spike could potentially cause havoc if it ever got to equip either of the Stinger Pingers. He also had plenty of fatties lined up for the late game, with Carrion Thrash, Jungle Weaver, Rock Sprite, and Gorger Wurm</v>

00:16:33.120 --> 00:17:03.430
<v Speaker 1>all providing a serious test to an opponent. Gregoire had only 12 monsters, highlighted by the bomb rare Flameblast Dragon, which could end games incredibly quickly. Amongst his spells, Double Blightning looked the most likely to cause inconvenience to opposing plans, plus some instant speed to the face old school action with Breath of Malfegor. To be honest though, with all those fat monsters lying in wait, it looked as if Mitamura might just be the favorite. It certainly looked that way in game one as Mitamura quickly blunted</v>

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:33.800
<v Speaker 1>regards early attacks with a Rock Sprute, gained control with a second large man in monstrous Carabid, and then used Vengeful Rebirth to take a one nil lead on the play. Gregor was forced into disastrous double Mulligan in game two and a reasonable start of Dragon fodder was rendered entirely ineffective by Mitamura finding and casting one of his Vithian Stinger. Gorger Worm was more than enough to set up the victory and without ever looking in trouble, Mitamura was on the verge of the semifinals where neither player could make early inroads.</v>

00:17:33.870 --> 00:18:06.070
<v Speaker 1>In game three, it looked as if Mitamura was destined for the sweep, but Turn 5 sent the game the Belgians way as Jund Hackblade was equipped with Lightning Talons and then a combination of Blightning and Breath of Malfegor burned away the remaining life, forcing a game four. Unfortunately for Gregor, that fourth game was back to business as usual with Carrion Thrash and then Rhox Brute giving Mitamura the edge. For the first time in the match, Gregoire found his Flameblast Dragon and looked to force a decider. However, vengeful Rebirth from Mitamura dealt with the dragon</v>

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:17.470
<v Speaker 1>and recovered his Carrion Thrash for an authentic double whammy. Down it came again, along with Gorger Wurm and Greg. Rob was out of answers. Mitamura 3. Gregoire one</v>

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:23.810
<v Speaker 1>quarterfinal 2 Conley Woods versus Brian Kibler.</v>

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:56.200
<v Speaker 1>Sitting next to his good friend Paul Reitzel, Kibler had aggressively gone in search of an Esper draft and had ended up with a concoction that didn't have a huge number of decent ways to win, but had a ton of ways to not lose, highlighted by a pair of wall of Denial plus sceptre of dominance. Woods meanwhile had been gifted some wonderful power in his packs, having opened Caldera Hellion, itself a board sweeper plus Fat Monster, he must have been delighted to find the astonishingly powerful Lavalanche from which there would really be a comeback.</v>

00:18:56.340 --> 00:19:26.730
<v Speaker 1>Also significant forwards was the main deck conclusion of Volcanic Submersion. Put in as a last card ahead of Trace of Abundance, Woods looked to multiple cyclists to fix his five colour manner base, and in retrospect that paid off for him multiple times, with Kibler starting the opener on just five cards. Woods in force the screw with Volcanic Submersion taking out Kibler's Mistvein Borderpost. Despite this, Kibler put up a strong showing, requiring Woods to deal with multiple threats. Unfortunately Woods had plenty of removal,</v>

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:57.740
<v Speaker 1>using Crystallisation to deal with a big Arsenal Thresher and Branching Bolt to do what it does best, create 2 for ones. Wall of denial and execution is Capture look to be allowing Kibler to stabilise and Woods felt it necessary to end things with the Uber rare Lavalanche, gifting Kibler the information for future games but exchanging that information for A1 nil lead. Kibler managed to gain the advantage in the second game, leaving Woods constantly on the back foot. With Woods down to just four life, Kibler was praying to avoid Lava Lunch,</v>

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:13.620
<v Speaker 1>but Wall of Denial, Pale Recluse, and then Ridge Rannet successfully clogged up the board. Although Kiba's deck looked on the face of it to be set up for the long game against Lavalanche, that wasn't the case, and for the second game running, Woods was able to bring out the bomb to seal the deal.</v>

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:44.110
<v Speaker 1>Kibler gave himself a chance in the third game when Brainbite took away Caldera Hellion, ensuring that his flies would have a chance of going the distance. Pale Recluse looked to stop that plan, but Kibler had the Executioner's Capsule ready to deal with that problem over and over again. Two Kibler monsters were met by one Wall of Denial, and Woods life dwindled away. While Kibler represented Traumatic Visions to counter the Lavalanche, it wasn't necessary with Woods unable to find the Rare this time. 21</v>

00:20:45.030 --> 00:21:07.040
<v Speaker 1>The fourth game, well, that was anticlimactic for Kibler. The highlight was Brainbite taking away Lavalanche, but that was all he could look to in a game that quickly got away from him. In a game that saw Woods draw creatures and Kibler draw lands, it was soon over. And with a deck packed full of bombs, Woods was off to the semifinals. Woods 3, Kibler 1.</v>

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:12.400
<v Speaker 1>Quarterfinal 3. Michael Hebgen versus Zach Hill.</v>

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:44.230
<v Speaker 1>Hedtke was the major Bant player at the final table. He had the highest creature counted, 18, and among his spells sat the fabulous Obelisk Avalara. The Obelisk couldn't do 5 ridiculous things for him, but three was going to be good times. The blue ability allowed him to look through his deck, drawing and discarding his way to card advantage turn after turn. The green ability was a massive pump to one of his monsters, which might well turn out to be flying and exalted, and if in trouble he could turn on the white ability,</v>

00:21:44.310 --> 00:22:14.260
<v Speaker 1>gaining him five life, which turn after turn would turn out to mean a lot. He'll meanwhile had gone into Esper from the start with Courier's capsule, Tao Gargoyle, and Executioners capsule defining his draft. When Hill opened Banefire, there was no way it was going into anyone else's pile and he made sure to play the game ender. There was no sign of the Banefire in the opener, while Hedtke found his Obelisk of Alara initially that allowed him to pull out of range of Hills Flyers by gaining 5 life turn,</v>

00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:23.520
<v Speaker 1>but when he started granting the green ability to his Valeron Outlander a massive threat against a succession of black permanence for Hill, the Czech player had a one nil lead</v>

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:54.650
<v Speaker 1>with Hapki Mulligan into five. In the second game. Hill was quick to take advantage with Esper Stormblade and Lich Lord of Unx taking chunks out of the check. Deny Reality returned a border post to the check hand and Cascade brought Hill a free Kathari Screecher, just compounding the problems for Hedke. There wasn't much more to say with Hill equalising soon after. Game 3 began with A33 fly for Hedke with Esper Cormorant, but Hill had Targaryen Gargoyle to trump that. Flies continued on both sides</v>

00:22:54.750 --> 00:23:26.040
<v Speaker 1>and we were into a classic matchup of threat and removal. In the end, Heckes Wave Skimmer Avon confronted Hills Kathari Screecher and that was not a fair fight with Hill. Eventually out of flies, the cheque took the lead once more. Back came Hill again with Hedtke allowing him back into the match via stumbling on manner, this time sticking at just three lands. It didn't help that the check man had misread a Death Baron, sending an otherwise viable blocker to the graveyard. Even so, the match seemed to favour Hedtke whenever he was able to</v>

00:23:26.120 --> 00:23:56.450
<v Speaker 1>actually combine land and spells. While mulligans or manna issues have contributed to both Hills game wins, Hill opened as the aggressor and soon had Hedtke on the ropes. But that was the key for Hedtke to bring out the mighty Obelisk of Alara once more. Although Five life pattern was a huge amount of slog through, it looked for a time as if he'll would manage it, but each time he drew a land against a creature for Hedtke, that hope diminished. Eventually, HEB he felt able to switch modes with the Obelisk, grinding through his deck with the blue ability</v>

00:23:56.550 --> 00:24:09.180
<v Speaker 1>and grinding his way through Hills attackers with the green pump ability. When he unearthed A Kathari Screecher, lethal damage was on the board and Hill was defeated. Hedtke 3. Hill 2</v>

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:14.990
<v Speaker 1>Quarterfinal 4 Paul Reitzel versus Tom Ross</v>

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:47.380
<v Speaker 1>Reitzel, sitting to Kiba's left, had correctly worked out that Kibler would want Esper come what may, and so quickly settled into Red Green, finally adding black. His highlights would be creatures, while the Latour Rocks Brute Cavern Thoctar carrying Thrash, and his undoubted star would be Mycoloth, A devouring monster of doom that could spew out huge quantities of tokens turn after turn. Add in a Hissing Iguana to punish opponents when the tokens were outmatched, and you had a decent deck for sure. Ross, meanwhile, had drafted Naya.</v>

00:24:47.530 --> 00:25:17.860
<v Speaker 1>He was looking to curve out with his 18 monsters, including Powerhouse One Drop while in a Cattle Banch, Sure Blade at 2, and an assortment of decent Exalted men that included a Craze and Squire and a pair of Rocks bodyguards. Neither player was tested in the opener, as Reitzel stumbled catastrophically on manner despite an opening that began promisingly, with Druid of the Anima and Elvish Visionary going in search of more land, Ross simply casts monsters. Reitzel didn't, and it was one nil. Things look much better for Reitzel</v>

00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:48.450
<v Speaker 1>in Game 2, as he made Michael off as an 884 tokens later it was gone, with Ross managing to find one of his few answers, Oblivion Ring. However, Hissing Iguanar tilted the game back towards Reitzel as the tokens once again had value even as they died. With both Rocks Bodyguards pressed into service, Ross was able to teeter precariously on the brink of safety for a while, but eventually Reitzel was able to send the Iguana into the red zone, knowing that even in death it would trigger for all the other lethal combatants that turn</v>

00:25:48.690 --> 00:26:18.680
<v Speaker 1>11. The defining moment of the third game came when Ross began with Forest and Wild Newcastle. As he commented afterwards, this was the second time he had first picked the Newcastle in draft, and it went a sizable way towards taking the game. Although Reitzel stabilised via a neat combination of Yoke of the Damned plus Skeletonize, Ross was ready to reload, first with Rocks Bodyguard and then with Asha's Favor. Tapping red and green, he used Colossal Might to make the bodyguard utterly gigantic</v>

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:48.780
<v Speaker 1>and Meets all swept them up, leaving him one down with two to play. Game 4 was your typical limited fare, a battle of attrition with creatures trading left and right, careful use of removal and instant combat changes, and an ever revolving board state that saw both players losing life at regular intervals. In these games, the winners generally the one with one more monster leftover than the other guy, and with Cavern Thoctar and Carrion Thrash playing two on one. That man was Paul Reitzel. A decider</v>

00:26:48.880 --> 00:27:14.570
<v Speaker 1>would be necessary. As for that decider, Ross wasn't in it Once Reitzel Head again used the combination of Yoke of the Damned and skeletonised to good effect. It didn't help that Ross was basically just drawing land, and although Reitzel was concerned about a possible martial coup, one of only a few conceivable outs, Ross had nothing and Reitzel was one step closer to the championship match. Reitzel 3, Ross 2.</v>

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:20.800
<v Speaker 1>Semifinal one Kazuya Mitamura versus Paul Reitzel.</v>

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:52.290
<v Speaker 1>Rizzo's reward for making it past Tom Ross was to face the most experienced top eighter left, the Japanese man Mitamura. Coming into the weekend with 92 lifetime pro points, Mitamura had now guaranteed himself to cross the 100 mark, a significant achievement so early in his pro career. Reitzel began the opener as the aggressor, but a delicious cascade of Bloodbraid Elf into Ember Weavers soon put the brakes on. The Weaver went down to Paul Sangrite Backlash, but again Mitamura steadied the ship with Rock Sprite</v>

00:27:52.430 --> 00:28:10.760
